Let $I$ be the incenter of $\triangle{ABC}$. Since $\overline{MI} \parallel \overline{BC}$ and $\overline{BI}$ is the angle bisector of $\angle{ABC}$, it follows that
\[\angle{MBI} = \angle{CBI} = \angle{MIB} = \frac{1}{2}\angle{MBC}\]
By alternate interior angles and base angles of isosceles triangles, $MI = MB$ and similarly, $NI = NC$. We calculate the perimeter of $\triangle{AMN}$:
\begin{align*}
AM + MN + NA &= AM + MI + NI + NA \\
&= AM + MB + NC + NA \\
&= AB + AC \\
&= 15 + 20 \\
&= \boxed{35}
\end{align*}

