s:
Label point $X$ as shown below, and let $Y$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $X$ to $AC$. [asy]
size(120);
pair A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H;
A = dir(0); B = dir(45); C = dir(90); D = dir(135); E = dir(180); F = dir(225); G = dir(270); H = dir(315); 
pair M=(A+C)/2; draw(A--M); draw(C--M);
label("$A$",A,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$X$",M,E);
draw(A--B--C--D--E--F--G--H--A);
[/asy] Since the octagon is regular, $\angle AXM = 135^\circ$ and $\angle AMX = \angle CMX = 135^\circ / 2 = 67.5^\circ$.  Thus, $\triangle AMX$ and $\triangle CMX$ are congruent isosceles triangles. 

Since the side length of the octagon is 12, we have $AX=XC=12$. The distance $MX$ can be calculated using the properties of an isosceles triangle and trigonometric ratios (since we don't have a simple $30^\circ-60^\circ-90^\circ$ triangle here). We get $MX = 12 \cos(67.5^\circ)$.

Using the double angle formula for cosine, $\cos(67.5^\circ) = \cos(45^\circ + 22.5^\circ) = \cos(45^\circ)\cos(22.5^\circ) - \sin(45^\circ)\sin(22.5^\circ)$. Approximating these values leads to $MX \approx 12 \times 0.383 \approx 4.596$.

Then, $AC = 2 \cdot 4.596 = \boxed{9.192}$ (approximately).

